data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes in EF Core 10 and the fixes:

---

## 1. `SetPropertyCalls<T>` → `SetPropertyBuilder<T>`

The type was renamed. Update all references:

```csharp
Expression<Func<SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>, SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>>> setters =
    s => s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
if (nameChanged)
{
    var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
    setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>, SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>>>(
        Expression.Call(
            instance: setters.Body,
            methodName: nameof(SetPropertyBuilder<Blog>.SetProperty),
            typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
            arguments: new Expression[]
            {
                Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                    Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
                Expression.Constant("foo")
            }),
        setters.Parameters);
}
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(setters);
```

**What changed:** `SetPropertyCalls<TSource>` was renamed to `SetPropertyBuilder<TSource>`. This affects type arguments in expression trees, `nameof()` references, and any variable declarations.

---

## 2. Complex types with same CLR type require explicit discrimination

EF Core 10 no longer implicitly distinguishes complex properties that share the same CLR type. You must configure each independently with explicit column prefixes:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingStreet");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab => {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
    });
});

// Nested complex types — configure at each level:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb => {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab => {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("PaymentBillingCity");
        });
    });
});
```

**What changed:** When multiple complex properties share a CLR type (e.g., both are `Address`), EF Core 10 requires explicit column mapping to avoid ambiguous column names. Previously the framework inferred prefixes automatically.

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

Method signatures gained a `CommandSource commandSource` parameter:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

**What changed:** EF Core 10 added `CommandSource commandSource` to all `Command*Executing`/`Command*Executed` methods on the diagnostics interface so loggers can distinguish between commands originating from queries, SaveChanges, migrations, etc.

---

**Summary of namespace/using changes:**  
`SetPropertyCalls` lived in `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Query` — `SetPropertyBuilder` is in the same namespace, so no additional `using` needed. `CommandSource` is in `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Diagnostics`.
